"Internal Error ()\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/main/LICENSE-MIT\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://raw.githubusercontent.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/main/LICENSE-MIT: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/commits/main/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/commits/main/: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - THU-HAS/MegaZK · GitHub (https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK\",\"pattern\":\"memory\"}); Total lines: 383\nL222: This repository is the open-source implementation of MegaZK, the system described in the ICS '26 paper 【101†MegaZK: A Memory Efficient GPU System Accelerating End-to-end Zero-Knowledge Proof†dl.acm.org】 (DOI: 【102†10.1145/3797905.3807842†doi.org】). Citation: Muyang Li, Yueteng Yu, Bangyan Wang, Xiong Fan, Mingyu Gao, and Shuwen Deng. 2026. In Proceedings of the 40th ACM International Conference on Supercomputing (ICS '26). ACM, 1165–1176. 【102†https://doi.org/10.1145/3797905.3807842†doi.org】\nL223: The GPU proving path lives in the forked 【103†`plonky2`】 crate and calls into 【104†`era-boojum-cuda`】 / 【105†`era-cuda`】 for CUDA kernels and runtime bindings.\nL224: \nL225: The default Cargo feature set is CPU-only and does not build CUDA dependencies. In the benchmark programs, `--baseline` selects the CPU prover; the GPU path is opt-in with `--features gpu`. Running a non-baseline benchmark without that feature returns `GPU proving is unavailable; rebuild with --features gpu`.\nL226: This monorepo is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0. See 【94†`LICENSE`】 and 【97†`NOTICE`】.\nL227: ## Repository Layout\nL228:   * 【103†`plonky2`】: forked Plonky2 workspace. GPU proving enters through `build_gpu` / `GpuCircuitData::prove` and the `plonky2/plonky2/src/integration` module.\nL229:   * 【104†`era-boojum-cuda`】: CUDA kernels and Rust wrappers used by the GPU prover.\nL230:   * 【105†`era-cuda`】: CUDA runtime bindings.\nL231:   * 【106†`plonky2-sha256`】, 【107†`plonky2-sha512`】, 【108†`plonky2-examples`】, 【109†`proto-neural-zkp`】: benchmark applications.\nL232:   * 【110†`plonky2_ed25519`】: experimental Ed25519 benchmark. Proof generation is expected to run, but verification currently has a known issue (`BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`). That is not a regression.\nL233:   * 【111†`plonky2-gpu-bench`】: std-only internal helper for shared benchmark timing, verification policy, and CUDA memory reporting.\nL234: ## Current Reproduction Environment\nL235: \nL236: The following environment is the current known reproduction baseline. Other versions may work, but have not been normalized for this open-source snapshot.\nL237:   * OS: Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS, Linux 6.8.0-124-generic, x86_64\nL238:   * Rust: `nightly-2023-11-28` (pinned in 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】)\nL239:     * `rustc 1.76.0-nightly (49b3924bd 2023-11-27)`\nL240:     * `cargo 1.76.0-nightly (9b13310ca 2023-11-24)`\nL241:   * CUDA toolkit: 12.x (this snapshot was built with 12.5, `nvcc V12.5.82`)\nL242:   * CMake: 3.30.3\nL243:   * clang: 14.0.0\nL244:   * GPU/driver: requires an NVIDIA GPU and driver compatible with CUDA 12.x. If CMake cannot detect the GPU architecture during build, set `CUDAARCHS` manually, for example `CUDAARCHS=89`.\nL245: The repository has a pinned toolchain in 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】. For CPU baseline comparisons, `RUSTFLAGS=-Ctarget-cpu=native` can improve performance on the local machine.\nL246: ## Support Matrix\nL247: \nL248: Benchmark  | GPU proof  | CPU baseline  | Verification status\nL249: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL250: Factorial  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL251: Fibonacci  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL252: SHA256  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL253: SHA512  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL254: ECDSA  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL255: MVM  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL256: Ed25519  | Experimental  | Experimental  | Known verification issue\nL258: ## Benchmark Circuit Sources\nL259: \nL260: These are the open-source circuit / gadget implementations used by each benchmark (not the proving-system libraries). Commands below are this repository's CLI; full smoke and paper-scale invocations are in 【112†Build And Run】. Paper numbers in brackets are ICS '26 camera-ready references. No pinned commit is recorded in this snapshot, so links go to the upstream repository root.\nL263:   * SHA256 — run: `-p plonky2_sha256` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit: 【116†polymerdao/plonky2-sha256】 (paper [30]; in-tree 【106†`plonky2-sha256/`】).\nL265:   * ECDSA — run: `-p plonky2-examples --example 4_ecdsa_verification` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit example: 【118†qope/plonky2-examples】 (paper [36]; in-tree 【108†`plonky2-examples/`】). ECDSA gadget used by that example: 【119†0xPolygonZero/plonky2-ecdsa】 (in-tree 【120†`plonky2-ecdsa/`】).\nL266:   * Ed25519 — run: `-p plonky2_ed25519` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit vendored in this tree: 【121†Electron-Labs/plonky2_ed25519】 (in-tree 【110†`plonky2_ed25519/`】). Paper [29] cites 【122†polymerdao/plonky2-ed25519】; the files here match Electron-Labs (authors, `curve25519-dalek` git rev, and layout), not the polymerdao tree.\nL267:   * MVM — run: `-p neural-zkp --bin rust-app` (for example `-- --input-size 100 --output-size 100`). Circuit: 【123†worldcoin/proto-neural-zkp】 (paper [43]; in-tree 【109†`proto-neural-zkp/`】). The vendored `Cargo.toml` still lists `https://github.com/dcbuild3r/proto-neural-zkp/` as `repository`; that is a contributor copy of the Worldcoin project.\nL268: ## Build And Run\nL269: \nL270: Run the following commands from the repository root.\nL271: \nL272: The default feature set is CPU-only. Use `--features gpu` for the GPU prover, and `--baseline` for the CPU prover in the benchmark CLIs.\nL273: \nL274: This is a root Cargo workspace. Commands below use `--locked` so they build from the checked-in root `Cargo.lock`. rustup will pick `nightly-2023-11-28` from 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】. GPU builds need CUDA 12.x, an NVIDIA driver, and (when CMake cannot detect the architecture) `CUDAARCHS`.\nL275: The commands in this README are this repository's CLI.\nL276: Every benchmark reports `Time taken to build:`, `Time taken to prove:`, and `Time taken to verify:` using Rust `Duration` formatting. GPU runs also report used and free CUDA memory with a shared format. MVM retains its existing seven-column CSV output in addition to these human-readable timings. A completed verification also emits exactly one `BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: PASS`, `BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`, or `BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: FAIL` machine-readable status line.\nL277: ### Smoke (small workloads)\nL278: \nL279: #### Factorial\nL280: \nL281:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example factorial -- --num 2000\nL282:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --example factorial -- --num 2000 --baseline\nL283: \nL284: #### Fibonacci\nL285: \nL286:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example fibonacci -- --num 2000\nL287:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --example fibonacci -- --num 2000 --baseline\nL288: #### SHA256\nL289: \nL290:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 --features gpu -- --num 1\nL291:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 -- --num 1 --baseline\nL292: \nL293: #### SHA512\nL294: \nL295:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 --features gpu -- --num 1\nL296:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 -- --num 1 --baseline\nL297: #### ECDSA\nL298: \nL299:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --features gpu --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 1\nL300:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 1 --baseline\nL301: #### Ed25519 Experimental\nL302: \nL303:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 --features gpu -- --num 1\nL304:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 -- --num 1 --baseline\nL305: \nL306: Ed25519 proof generation is expected to run. A verification failure after proof generation is a known issue (`BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`) and should not be treated as a regression.\nL307: #### MVM\nL308: \nL309:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --features gpu --bin rust-app -- --input-size 100 --output-size 100\nL310:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --bin rust-app -- --input-size 100 --output-size 100 --baseline\nL311: ### Paper-scale examples\nL312: \nL313: `--memory-profile` defaults to `compressed`. Paper-scale runs need large GPU memory (about 24GB class). Factorial and Fibonacci paper-scale sorting is very slow; prove can still run.\nL314: Uncompressed (paper-U size flags):\nL315: \nL316:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example factorial -- --num 131073 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL317:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example fibonacci -- --num 262143 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL318:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 --features gpu -- --num 60 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL319:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 --features gpu -- --num 20 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL320:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --features gpu --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 6 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile uncompressed\nL321:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 --features gpu -- --num 2 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL322:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --features gpu --bin rust-app -- --input-size 4000 --output-size 4000 --coefficient-bits 16 --num-wires 400 --num-routed-wires 400 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile uncompressed\nL323: Compressed (paper-C size flags; default profile is already `compressed`):\nL324: \nL325:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example factorial -- --num 524289 --memory-profile compressed\nL326:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example fibonacci -- --num 1048575 --memory-profile compressed\nL327:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 --features gpu -- --num 210 --memory-profile compressed\nL328:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 --features gpu -- --num 137 --memory-profile compressed\nL329:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --features gpu --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 42 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile compressed\nL330:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 --features gpu -- --num 11 --memory-profile compressed\nL331:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --features gpu --bin rust-app -- --input-size 9000 --output-size 9000 --coefficient-bits 16 --num-wires 400 --num-routed-wires 400 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile compressed\nL332: Ed25519 paper-scale: prove is expected to run; verify remains the same known failure (`KNOWN_ISSUE`).\nL333: ## Notes For Contributors\nL334:   * This repository is a root Cargo workspace. Use `--locked` for reproducible builds against the checked-in root `Cargo.lock`.\nL335:   * The `plonky2` crate and benchmark packages keep CUDA support behind their non-default `gpu` feature. Targeted default builds are CPU-only.\nL336:   * The GPU path uses `CircuitBuilder::build_gpu` with witness input targets, followed by `GpuCircuitData::prove` with an equally sized witness input slice. GPU proofs continue to use the CPU verifier through `GpuCircuitData::verify`.\nL337:   * Verbose proving features may produce JSON debug dumps. These files are local artifacts and should not be committed.\nL338: ## Acknowledgments\nL339: \nL340: Proving-system libraries vendored in this tree (not the benchmark circuits above). Details are in 【97†`NOTICE`】:\nL341: \nL342:   * plonky2: 【113†0xPolygonZero/plonky2】 (also 【124†mir-protocol/plonky2】); this copy lives in 【103†`plonky2/`】\nL343:   * era-boojum: 【125†matter-labs/era-boojum】 (【126†`era-boojum/`】)\nL344:   * era-cuda: 【127†matter-labs/era-cuda】 (【105†`era-cuda/`】)\nL345:   * era-boojum-cuda: 【128†matter-labs/era-boojum-cuda】 (【104†`era-boojum-cuda/`】)\nL346: ## About\nL347: \nL348: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL349: \nL350: ### Resources\nL351: \nL352: 【71†Readme】\nL353: \nL354: License and 2 other licenses found\nL355: \nL356: 【72†Activity】\nL357: \nL358: 【73†Custom properties】\nL359: \nL360: ### Stars\nL361: \nL362: 1 star\nL363: \nL364: ### Watchers\nL365: \nL366: 0 watching\nL367: \nL368: ### Forks\nL369: \nL370: 【74†0 forks】\nL371: \nL372: 【75†Report repository】\nL373: \nL374: ## Releases\nL375: \nL376: ## Packages\nL377: \nL378: ## Contributors\nL379: \nL380: ## Languages\nL381: \nL382: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck · GitHub (https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck\",\"pattern\":\"Goldilocks\"}); Total lines: 390\nL255: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $\\deg_{x_i}(p) \\leq d$. The user implements `RoundPolyEvaluator` to define per-pair round polynomial contributions; the library handles iteration, parallelism, and reductions.\nL256: \nL257:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL258:     use effsc::provers::coefficient::CoefficientProver;\nL259: \nL260:     let mut prover = CoefficientProver::new(\nL261:         &evaluator,\nL262:         tablewise,\nL263:         pairwise,\nL264:     );\nL265:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL266:         &mut prover,\nL267:         num_rounds,\nL268:         &mut transcript,\nL269:         noop_hook,\nL270:     );\nL271: ### Verification\nL272: One verifier for any degree $d$. Returns `SumcheckResult { challenges, final_claim }` — ⚠️ the caller is responsible for the oracle check (【97†Thaler Remark 4.2†people.cs.georgetown.edu】).\nL273: \nL274:     use effsc::{noop_hook_verify, verifier::sumcheck_verify};\nL275: \nL276:     let result = sumcheck_verify(\nL277:         claimed_sum,\nL278:         degree,\nL279:         num_rounds,\nL280:         &mut transcript,\nL281:         noop_hook_verify,\nL282:     )?;\nL283: \nL284:     // Standalone: compare against the prover's claimed final value.\nL285:     assert_eq!(result.final_claim, proof.final_value);\nL286: \nL287:     // Composed (WHIR, GKR): pass final_claim to the next layer.\nL288:     next_layer_claim = result.final_claim;\nL289: ## Variable Ordering\nL290: \nL291: Each prover comes in two variants:\nL292: \nL293:   * MSB (half-split) — optimal memory layout in most cases. Used by WHIR and WARP.\nL294:   * LSB (pair-split) — optimal for streaming applications where evaluations arrive sequentially.\nL295: MSB  | LSB\nL296: --- | ---\nL297: `MultilinearProver`  | `MultilinearProverLSB`\nL298: `InnerProductProver`  | `InnerProductProverLSB`\nL299: `CoefficientProver`  | `CoefficientProverLSB`\nL300: `EqFactoredProver`  | —\nL301: `GkrProver`  | —\nL302: \nL303: See 【98†`docs/design.md`】 for details.\nL304: ## Partial Execution and Hooks\nL305: \nL306: The `sumcheck()` runner supports partial execution (`num_rounds < v`) and per-round hooks for composed protocols:\nL307: \nL308:     // WHIR: partial sumcheck with proof-of-work grinding\nL309:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL310:         &mut prover,\nL311:         folding_factor,  // num_rounds < v\nL312:         &mut transcript,\nL313:         |_, t| round_pow.prove(t),  // per-round hook\nL314:     );\nL315: ## SIMD Acceleration\nL316: \nL317: All provers transparently auto-dispatch to SIMD backends. Supported fields:\nL318: \nL319:   * [Input] Goldilocks ($p = 2^{64} - 2^{32} + 1$) and degree-2/3 extensions\nL320:   * [Input] M31 ($p = 2^{31} - 1$) and extensions\nL321:   * [Input] BabyBear ($p = 2^{31} - 2^{27} + 1$) and extensions\nL322:   * [Input] KoalaBear ($p = 2^{31} - 2^{24} + 1$) and extensions\nL323: Backend  | Width  | Platform\nL324: --- | --- | ---\nL325: NEON  | 2-wide  | aarch64 (Apple M-series, Graviton)\nL326: AVX-512 IFMA  | 8-wide  | x86_64 (Sapphire Rapids)\nL327: Scalar for other fields. See 【99†`SECURITY.md`】.\nL328: ## Examples\nL329: \nL330: Integrated into Whir (【100†PR】) and Warp (【101†PR】) with measured performance improvements. Integration capability for streaming contexts like 【102†Jolt】 is described in 【98†`docs/design.md`】.\nL331: \nL332: ## Correctness\nL333: \nL334: 🚧 Undergoing fuzzing over randomized inputs against 【103†z-tech/sumcheck-lean4】, an oracle with machine-checked proofs of completeness and soundness. Findings to follow.\nL335: ## References\nL336: \nL337: [【104†LFKN92†dl.acm.org】]: Carsten Lund, Lance Fortnow, Howard J. Karloff, and Noam Nisan. \"Algebraic Methods for Interactive Proof Systems\". In: Journal of the ACM 39.4 (1992).\nL338: \nL339: [【105†CTY11†arxiv.org】]: Graham Cormode, Justin Thaler, and Ke Yi. \"Verifying computations with streaming interactive proofs\". In: Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment 5.1 (2011), pp. 25-36.\nL340: [【106†VSBW1